WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
This is The Spread, your energy
markets briefing for Monday,

2
00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:07.280
the second of November. This
episode is written with AI,

3
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.080
and read by an AI clone of my voice.

4
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.760
A liquefied natural gas carrier that
Iran had cleared for passage was

5
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.160
attacked near the Strait of Hormuz.

6
00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:19.680
Separately, a tanker in the strait
has been hit by limpet mines for the

7
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.440
first time in the war, which means
neither permission nor routing is

8
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:26.293
a reliable shield for
Gulf cargoes right now.

9
00:00:26.347 --> 00:00:30.080
On the markets. Brent was trading
around one hundred and eighteen dollars

10
00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.520
a barrel on Friday. Front-month gas
at the Dutch Title Transfer Facility,

11
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.693
or TTF, went above one hundred
and one euros a megawatt hour,

12
00:00:38.747 --> 00:00:41.080
the first time since twenty twenty-two.

13
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.640
European gas storage stood at
seventy-four point six percent full as

14
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:46.760
of the thirty-first of October.

15
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.800
Starting in the Gulf. Bloomberg
reported that Pakistan had negotiated

16
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:54.720
safe passage with Iran for the carrier
Al Mayeda before it was attacked

17
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.360
near Hormuz. The report doesn't
say who carried out the attack,

18
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.520
or how badly the ship was damaged.

19
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.440
The point for buyers is simple.

20
00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.307
If a cargo cleared by
Tehran can still be hit,

21
00:01:05.333 --> 00:01:07.960
there's no dependable
way to buy safe transit,

22
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.013
and one workaround for
moving Gulf gas is gone.

23
00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:13.040
The tactics are shifting too.

24
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:17.280
Late on Thursday the twenty-ninth, a
small boat fixed two explosive devices

25
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.280
to the hull of the Louise 1,
according to Splash twenty-four seven.

26
00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:24.960
She's a Panama-flagged tanker of about
a hundred and sixty thousand deadweight

27
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:29.387
tonnes, and was hit some twenty-five
nautical miles northeast of Khasab.

28
00:01:29.413 --> 00:01:33.760
The blasts started an engine-room fire
and temporarily disabled the ship.

29
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.920
Maritime security sources told gCaptain
it's the first successful limpet-mine

30
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.933
attack in the strait since
the war began in February.

31
00:01:40.987 --> 00:01:44.560
The UK Maritime Trade Operations
centre logged it as the third report

32
00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:47.160
that day of an outbound tanker on fire.

33
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.840
On Friday, Iran's Islamic Revolutionary
Guard Corps Navy said four tankers

34
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.520
had caught fire and exploded while
using what it called unauthorised

35
00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.360
routes. That is Iran's claim alone.

36
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.640
No owner, flag state or
navy has confirmed it.

37
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.080
Turning to gas. TTF was up four
percent by mid-morning on Friday and

38
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.360
on course for an eight
percent weekly gain,

39
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.240
according to OilPrice dot com.

40
00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.160
Traders are pricing the risk that
Qatari and Emirati cargoes stay choked

41
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.720
off at Hormuz just as winter
starts with storage low.

42
00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.720
And storage has started to fall.

43
00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:23.520
Gas Infrastructure Europe data put
it at seventy-five percent on the

44
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.440
twenty-seventh of October, slipping
to today's level by month-end.

45
00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:31.920
Refiners feel this directly, through
higher costs for fuel and hydrogen,

46
00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.880
while switching from gas to oil
lends support to diesel and fuel oil.

47
00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:41.040
In Asia, ICIS expects Japan to
overtake China as the largest importer

48
00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:45.680
this year. That's sixty-two million
tonnes against sixty-one million.

49
00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.760
High prices and comfortable storage
are holding back Chinese buying.

50
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.920
On the supply side, Reuters reported
that Shell has taken the first long-term

51
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.120
contract cargo from Venture
Global's Plaquemines plant,

52
00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.440
now that its first phase is commissioned.

53
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:03.520
Over to crude. Brent reached one hundred
and seventeen dollars on Thursday

54
00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.040
after the Louise 1 attack,
according to OilPrice dot com.

55
00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.200
By Friday it was heading for a monthly
rise of thirteen to fourteen percent,

56
00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.520
its fourth straight monthly gain.

57
00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.360
Several things are holding it up.

58
00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:19.120
President Trump has repeatedly
suggested strikes on Iran could resume

59
00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:23.360
after the midterms. Explosions have
been reported at Iran's Jask port,

60
00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:28.000
but not confirmed. And the bank ANZ
raised its end-of-year Brent forecast

61
00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:31.760
by ten dollars, to one hundred and
five, assuming today's export and

62
00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.080
shipping limits last through December.

63
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:38.720
The Houthis also claim fresh strikes on
the Ras Tanura and Rabigh refineries,

64
00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.440
and on an Aramco facility at Abqaiq.

65
00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.480
Riyadh hasn't confirmed
any attack or damage.

66
00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:49.120
As expected, seven OPEC+ members left
December targets unchanged at Sunday's

67
00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.120
meeting, World Oil reported.

68
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.200
In practice it changes little.

69
00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.440
Saudi Arabia, Kuwait and Iraq are still
producing well below quota because

70
00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:58.720
of Hormuz.

71
00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:02.480
On diesel, Bloomberg reported that
Russia has extended its general ban

72
00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:05.440
on exports by producers
to the end of January,

73
00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.573
though some volumes may still flow.

74
00:04:07.627 --> 00:04:11.200
That leaves Turkey, Brazil and African
buyers competing with Europe for

75
00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.400
American and Indian barrels.

76
00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.120
American diesel isn't
making it to Europe either.

77
00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:18.520
In a note dated the
twenty-fifth of October,

78
00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:22.240
Kpler put spot freight from the US
Gulf Coast to northwest Europe at

79
00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.480
Worldscale four hundred and
sixty-five, the highest since April.

80
00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.040
That kept transatlantic
arbitrage shut for a second week.

81
00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.280
The route from India's west coast
to the Mediterranean was about four

82
00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.560
dollars a tonne from opening at the time.

83
00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.360
So Indian cargoes could well be first
to move if Mediterranean freight

84
00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:40.120
eases.

85
00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:44.400
And in earnings, third-quarter results
out on Friday show refining carrying

86
00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.800
the majors, according to OilPrice dot com.

87
00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.800
ExxonMobil made fifteen
point four billion dollars,

88
00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.760
with its Gulf Coast refineries
running at full capacity.

89
00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.160
Chevron earned nine point nine
billion, and its downstream arm brought

90
00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:02.080
in just under five billion, against
just over one billion a year ago.

91
00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:07.280
BP's underlying replacement cost profit
was eight point six billion dollars,

92
00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.440
nearly four times last year's.

93
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.240
Freight is showing up on the cost side.

94
00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:16.000
Splash twenty-four seven reported
a non-cash rise of five point four

95
00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.440
billion in Shell's
shipping lease liabilities.

96
00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:23.760
A few more things. Iran said action
against Sinokor tankers would start

97
00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:27.920
on the first of November, but nothing
has surfaced in today's reports.

98
00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:31.200
There's no word yet on whether the
owner of the Russian diesel tanker

99
00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:35.200
Agioi Fanendes named a buyer by
Senator Blumenthal's deadline of the

100
00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.920
thirty-first. And in France, TotalEnergies'
fuel price cuts and the state

101
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.600
reserve release haven't yet
been reported as starting.

102
00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:47.680
What to watch next. Tuesday
brings the US midterms.

103
00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:51.360
On Wednesday, the EIA weekly for the
week to the thirtieth of October.

104
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:55.600
Through the eleventh, the daily European
storage readings as the drawdown

105
00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:00.160
gets going. And on Saturday the
Agioi Fanendes is due in New York and

106
00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:00.800
New Jersey.

107
00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:05.760
Further out: Guyana's first
Uaru cargo in mid-December,

108
00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.600
then the end of Russia's diesel ban
and the EU's ban on long-term Russian

109
00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:11.200
gas imports in the new year.

110
00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:15.840
That's the brief. Sources for everything
you heard are in the show notes.
